<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albert Einstein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1879">1879</a>–<a href="/years/1955">1955</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albert Einstein was a German-born physicist, born in Ulm, whose theories of special and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theory-of-relativity">general relativity</a> transformed physics. In 1905, while working at the Swiss patent office in Bern, he published papers on relativity, light quanta and Brownian motion, and he received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physics for 1921. Of Jewish origin, he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1933 and settled in Princeton in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-einstein.jpg" alt="Albert Einstein" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oren Jack Turner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albert_Einstein_Head_cleaned.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Einstein was born in Ulm on 14 March 1879, a subject of the Kingdom of Württemberg in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>. His parents, Hermann Einstein and Pauline Koch, were secular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkenazi-jews">Ashkenazi Jews</a>. The family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> in 1880, where his father and uncle Jakob founded a company making electrical equipment. His parents worried that he had a learning disability because he was slow to talk, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">compass</a> his father gave him when he was five began a lifelong fascination with electromagnetism. He found the rote learning of the Luitpold Gymnasium harmful to creativity and left it to join his family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>He renounced his German citizenship on 28 January 1896, avoiding conscription, and enrolled that year at the Swiss federal polytechnic school in Zurich, graduating in 1900. He gained Swiss citizenship a year later and secured a permanent position at the Swiss Patent Office in Bern.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His first paper, on capillary attraction, appeared in 1901. In 1905, sometimes called his annus mirabilis, he published papers on the photoelectric effect, on Brownian motion, which showed that the movement could be taken as firm evidence that molecules exist, and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theory-of-relativity">special relativity</a>, and demonstrated that mass and energy are equivalent. The paper on the electrodynamics of moving bodies was received on 30 June 1905 and reconciled Maxwell's equations with Newtonian mechanics.</p><p>In 1915 he proposed a general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theory-of-relativity">theory of relativity</a> that extended his mechanics to gravitation, and a paper of the following year introduced the cosmological constant and is regarded as the first step in modern theoretical cosmology. In 1917 he introduced spontaneous and stimulated emission, the latter the core mechanism behind the laser. In his last years he objected to the randomness of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quantum-mechanics">quantum theory</a> and tried unsuccessfully to devise a unified field theory, which left him increasingly isolated from mainstream physics.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1914 Einstein moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, and in 1917 he became director of the Kaiser Wilhelm Institute for Physics. He was visiting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1933 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> came to power; horrified by the Nazi persecution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, he stayed, and became an American citizen in 1940. He died on 18 April 1955.</p><p>The Nobel committee found that no nomination in 1921 met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-nobel">Alfred Nobel</a>'s criteria, so the prize in physics was carried forward and awarded to Einstein for his discovery of the law of the photoelectric effect. The element einsteinium was named after him in 1955, and Time named him Person of the Century in 1999.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Albert Einstein” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Albert Einstein</b> was born in Ulm in 1879, though his family soon moved to Munich.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Federal Polytechnic School in Zurich, founded in 1855 and now ETH Zurich, became one of the leading technical universities, and in 1905 <b>Albert Einstein</b>, then a clerk at the patent office in Bern, published his papers on relativity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Bertrand Russell argued that it weakens the individual’s capacity to judge his country’s foreign policy; <b>Albert Einstein</b> called it “an infantile disease … the measles of mankind”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>The economist Alexander Sachs delivered to Franklin Roosevelt a letter signed by <b>Albert Einstein</b>, warning that Germany might build a uranium bomb.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-11">11 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The minster spire was completed in 1890, and <b>Albert Einstein</b> was born in Ulm in 1879.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-ulm">Free Imperial City of Ulm</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ulm, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Princeton, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.4,9.99&amp;year=1928" aria-label="Ulm on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4782 471 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Albert Einstein"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5232 741h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5232 741h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.4,9.99&amp;year=1928">What was here in 1928? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;As far as the laws of mathematics refer to reality, they are not certain; and as far as they are certain, they do not refer to reality.&#8221;</p><footer>Geometry and Experience, address to the Prussian Academy of Sciences (1921) &#183; tr. G. B. Jeffery and W. Perrett, 1922 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Geometry_and_Experience" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;No one must think that Newton's great creation can be overthrown in any real sense by this or by any other theory.&#8221;</p><footer>Time, Space, and Gravitation, The Times, 28 November 1919 &#183; tr. for The Times, 1919 (translator not named) &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Time,_Space,_and_Gravitation" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The great attraction of the theory is its logical consistency. If any deduction from it should prove untenable, it must be given up. A modification of it seems impossible without destruction of the whole.&#8221;</p><footer>Time, Space, and Gravitation, The Times, 28 November 1919 &#183; tr. for The Times, 1919 (translator not named) &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Time,_Space,_and_Gravitation" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I adhered scrupulously to the precept of that brilliant theoretical physicist L. Boltzmann, according to whom matters of elegance ought to be left to the tailor and to the cobbler.&#8221;</p><footer>Relativity: The Special and General Theory (1916), preface &#183; tr. Robert W. Lawson, 1920 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5001/pg5001-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;No fairer destiny could be allotted to any physical theory, than that it should of itself point out the way to the introduction of a more comprehensive theory, in which it lives on as a limiting case.&#8221;</p><footer>Relativity: The Special and General Theory (1916), ch. 22 &#183; tr. Robert W. Lawson, 1920 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5001/pg5001-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;At this point an enigma presents itself which in all ages has agitated inquiring minds. How can it be that mathematics, being after all a product of human thought which is independent of experience, is so admirably appropriate to the objects of reality?&#8221;</p><footer>Geometry and Experience, address to the Prussian Academy of Sciences (1921) &#183; tr. G. B. Jeffery and W. Perrett, 1922 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7333/pg7333-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
